 "licence_note": null,
 "history": "Every woman of the 1750s wore stays over her shift: boned, giving the period's smooth conical torso, most laced behind; dark green, blue or white were the common covers (the Fort Ticonderoga guidelines) [S6]. Working women also wore them, or a lightly boned bodice, as the outer garment over the shift with a petticoat and apron (Goschenhoppen Historians on rural Pennsylvania German dress) [S9]. Front lacing over a stomacher, as in the German Mieder, is less certain for the Mohawk valley (evidence L to M).",
 "sources": [
